ecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37198076"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.amjcard.2023.04.025","issn":["00029149","18791913"],"issn_org":["00029149"],"journal":"American Journal of Cardiology","pages":"100-109","publisher":"Elsevier Inc.","status":"p","title":"American Journal of Cardiology","title_org":"The American Journal of Cardiology","volume":"199"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85159379930"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-26e75f0b-8baa-4fea-a1f4-3c4a436d3d5d","sdu-1162c9d7-3002-434d-90cf-82e7fb0291e3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Coronary Volume to Left Ventricular Mass Ratio in Patients With Hypertension","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.46-672586","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Background The prognostic value of coronary CT angiography (CTA)-derived fractional flow reserve (FFR) beyond 1-year outcomes and in patients with high levels of coronary artery calcium (CAC) is uncertain. Purpose To assess the prognostic value of coronary CTA-derived FFR test results on 3-year clinical outcomes in patients with coronary stenosis and among a subgroup of patients with high levels of CAC. Materials and Methods This study represents a 3-year follow-up of patients with new-onset stable angina pectoris who were consecutively enrolled in the Assessing Diagnostic Value of Noninvasive CT-FFR in Coronary Care, known as ADVANCE (ClinicalTrials.gov: NCT02499679) registry, between December 2015 and October 2017 at three Danish sites. A high CAC was defined as an Agatston score of at least 400. A lesion-specific coronary CTA-derived FFR value of 2 cm with distal-to-stenosis value at or below 0.80 represented an abnormal test result. The primary end point was a composite of all-cause death and nonfatal spontaneous myocardial infarction. Event rates were estimated using the one-sample binomial model, and relative risk was compared between participants stratified by results of coronary CTA-derived FFR. Results This study included 900 participants: 523 participants with normal results (mean age, 64 years ± 9.6 [SD]; 318 male participants) and 377 with abnormal results from coronary CTA-derived FFR (mean age, 65 years ± 9.6; 264 male participants). The primary end point occurred in 11 of 523 (2.1%) and 25 of 377 (6.6%) participants with normal and abnormal coronary CTA-derived FFR results, respectively (relative risk, 3.1; 95% CI: 1.6, 6.3; P < .001). In participants with high CAC, the primary end point occurred in four of 182 (2.2%) and 19 of 212 (9.0%) participants with normal and abnormal coronary CTA-derived FFR results, respectively (relative risk, 4.1; 95% CI: 1.4, 11.8; P = .001). Conclusion In individuals with stable angina, a normal coronary CTA-derived FFR test result identified participants with a low 3-year risk of all-cause death or nonfatal spontaneous myocardial infarction, both in the overall cohort and in participants with high CAC scores. Clinical trial registration no. NCT02499679 Published under a CC BY 4.0 license. Supplemental material is available for this article. See also the editorial by Sinitsyn in this issue.","author_count":16,"contributors":["Madsen, Kristian T","Nørgaard, Bjarne Linde","Øvrehus, Kristian A","Jensen, Jesper Møller","Parner, Erik Thorlund","Grove, Erik Lerkevang","Fairbairn, Timothy A","Nieman, Koen","Patel, Manesh R","Rogers, Campbell","Mullen, Sarah","Mickley, Hans","Rohold, Allan","Bøtker, Hans Erik","Leipsic, Jonathon","Sand, Niels Peter R"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-691931"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Madsen, Kristian T","Madsen, Kristian Tækker"],"country":["Canada","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Duke University","HeartFlow Inc.","Liverpool Heart and Chest Hospital","Stanford University","University of British Columbia (UBC)"],"keyword":["Aged","Angina Stable diagnostic imaging","Calcium","Computed Tomography Angiography","Coronary Angiography","Fractional Flow Reserve Myocardial","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Myocardial Infarction","Prognosis","Tomography X Ray Computed"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"prognostic value of coronary ct angiography derived fractional flow reserve on 000000000003 year outcomes in patients with stable angina","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","