Inc.","status":"p","title":"Journal of Cardiovascular Computed Tomography","volume":"16"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85114747899"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-ce2482df-eaf4-455c-9990-9d5e35db50d5","sdu-408c03c3-1482-408f-8ec0-059a0d38eb66"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"<i>Trans</i>-lesional fractional flow reserve gradient as derived from coronary CT improves patient management : ADVANCE registry","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-027632","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Objective. To explore anxiety and self-isolation in patients with inflammatory rheumatic disease (IRD)15 months into the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic, including attitudes toward and effects of SARS-CoV-2 vaccination. Methods. A nationwide online survey was conducted at 3 timepoints: May 2020, November 2020, and May 2021. Patients with IRD followed in the Danish Rheumatology Quality Registry (DANBIO) were asked about the effects of the pandemic, including SARS-CoV-2 infection and their behavior, anxiety, and concerns. The May 2021 survey included attitudes toward SARS-CoV-2 and influenza vaccination. Characteristics associated with self-isolation in May 2021 were explored with adjusted logistic regression analyses that included patient characteristics and SARS-CoV-2 vaccination status. Results. Respondents to surveys 1, 2, and 3 included 12,789; 14,755; and 13,921 patients, respectively; 64% had rheumatoid arthritis and 63% were female. Anxiety and concerns were highest in May 2020 and decreased to stable levels in November 2020 and May 2021; 86%, 50%, and 52% of respondents reported self-isolation, respectively. In May 2021, 4% of respondents self-reported previous SARS-CoV-2 infection. The SARS-CoV-2 vaccine acceptance rate was 86%, and the proportion of patients vaccinated against influenza had increased from 50% in winter 2019-2020 to 64% in winter 2020-2021. The proportion of patients with anxiety appeared similar among those vaccinated and unvaccinated against SARS-CoV-2. In multivariable analyses, being unvaccinated, female gender, receiving biologic drugs, and poor quality of life were independently associated with self-isolation. Conclusion. Levels of anxiety and self-isolation decreased after the initial lockdown period in patients with IRD. Half of the patients reported self-isolation in May 2021, a phase that included widespread reopening of society and large-scale vaccination. The lack of prepandemic data prevented a full understanding of the long-term effects of the pandemic on anxiety and self-isolation in patients with IRD.","author_count":21,"contributors":["Glintborg, Bente","Jensen, Dorte Vendelbo","Terslev, Lene","Hendricks, Oliver","Østergaard, Mikkel","Rasmussen, Simon Horskjær","Pfeiffer Jensen, Mogens","Adelsten, Thomas","Colic, Ada","Danebod, Kamilla","Kildemand, Malene","Loft, Anne Gitte","Munk, Heidi Lausten","Pedersen, Jens Kristian","Østgård, René Drage","Sørensen, Christian Møller","Krogh, Niels Steen","Agerbo, Jette Nørgaard","Ziegler, Connie","Hetland, Merete Lund"],"cuid":["S03-092.52-014464"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Glintborg, Bente"],"keyword":["Arthritis Rheumatoid","Autoimmune Diseases","Biological Products","COVID 19 Vaccines therapeutic use","COVID 19 prevention control","Communicable Disease Control","Disease Outbreaks","Female","Humans","Influenza Human epidemiology","Male","Pandemics prevention control","Quality of Life","Registries","Rheumatic diseases","SARS CoV 2","Vaccination","Vaccines"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"OT":["Danish Rheumatism Association"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"long term behavioral changes during the covid 000000000019 pandemic and impact of vaccination in patients with inflammatory rheumatic diseases","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3899/jrheum.211280","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-092.52-01