{"abstract":"Objectives: Patients with haematological disorders may be particularly vulnerable to respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) infection; however, this is unknown. Methods: We conducted a prospective, nationwide study including 66 patients in follow-up at Danish haematology departments with a malignant or non-malignant haematological disorder and with verified SARS-CoV-2 infection. Outcomes were intensive care unit (ICU) admission and one-month survival rate. Results: Mean age was 66.7 years, 60.6% were males, 90.9% had comorbidity, and 13.6% had a BMI ≥ 30. The most frequent diagnoses were chronic lymphocytic leukaemia/lymphoma (47.0%), multiple myeloma (16.7%) and acute leukaemia/myelodysplastic syndrome (AL/MDS) (12.1%). Treatment for the haematological disease was ongoing in 59.1% of cases. Neutropenia was present in 6.5%, lymphopenia in 46.6% and hypogammaglobulinaemia in 26.3%. The SARS-CoV-2 infection was mild in 50.0%, severe in 36.4% and critical in 13.6%. After one month, 21.2% had been admitted to ICU, and 24.2% died. Mortality was highest in older patients, patients with severe/critical SARS-CoV-2 infection, high comorbidity score or high performance status score, purine analogue treatment and with AL/MDS. Although older patients and patients with comorbidities had the highest mortality rates, mortality was considerable among all haematological patients. Conclusion: Haematological patients with SARS-CoV-2 infection has a severe clinical course.","author_count":24,"contributors":["Glenthøj, Andreas","Jakobsen, Lasse Hjort","Sengeløv, Henrik","Ahmad, Syed A","Qvist, Kristian","Rewes, Annika","Poulsen, Christian B","Overgaard, Ulrik M","Mølle, Ingolf","Severinsen, Marianne Tang","Strandholdt, Casper N","Maibom, Jack","Kodahl, Annette R","Ryg, Jesper","Ravn, Pernille","Johansen, Isik S","Helso, Soren Niemi","Jensen-Fangel, Søren","Kisielewicz, Jacek","Wiese, Lothar","Helleberg, Marie","Kirk, Ole","Clausen, Michael R","Frederiksen, Henrik"],"cuid":["S04-095.76-009237"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"corresponding_author":["Frederiksen, Henrik"],"keyword":["immunology and infectious diseases"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sars cov 000000000002 infection among patients with haematological disorders severity and one month outcome in 000000000066 danish patients in a nationwide cohort study","src":["aau","au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/333311080/oa_ejh.13519.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S04-095.76-009237","identifier":[{"id":"32939853","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85091686657","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-bf984566-8b1f-4079-a889-ee37e1a903cd","au-1b2d96a5-dd92-4704-bc27-c61c6545834a","ku-5f329731-3349-4c25-9ce1-3a98ae3f86b2","sdu-79709b70-51e1-4446-88a5-b5f7135ff68d"],"keyword":["immunology and infectious diseases"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-7-4550F4843B63A7B069E642CBA218EFB3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/333311080/oa_ejh.13519.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/174538729/ejh.13519.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/424057877/SARS_CoV_2_infection_among_patients_with_haematological_disorders.pdf","ver